WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.924
Node.js distinguishes between direct
process creation and shell interpretation.

2
00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:07.621
The exec API runs a command string through
a shell.

3
00:00:07.701 --> 00:00:12.001
By contrast, execFile launches the
specified executable directly by default and

4
00:00:12.081 --> 00:00:13.950
accepts a separate argument array.

5
00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:18.392
Spawn also launches a command with
discrete arguments unless its options request a

6
00:00:18.472 --> 00:00:18.846
shell.

7
00:00:18.926 --> 00:00:22.477
Direct execution removes the
shell-language parser from the path,

8
00:00:22.557 --> 00:00:26.047
so input cannot become pipelines,
redirections, substitutions,

9
00:00:26.127 --> 00:00:28.744
or additional shell commands through that
layer.

10
00:00:28.824 --> 00:00:32.999
A safer wrapper therefore specifies one
application-controlled executable,

11
00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:36.132
constructs an argument array, and leaves
shell disabled.

12
00:00:36.212 --> 00:00:39.141
This also avoids relying on complicated
quoting rules.

13
00:00:39.221 --> 00:00:43.022
Shell syntax varies between POSIX shells,
Windows command processors,

14
00:00:43.102 --> 00:00:47.214
and PowerShell, while nested tools can
introduce additional parsing layers.

15
00:00:47.294 --> 00:00:51.780
A string that appears correctly quoted for
one environment may behave differently in

16
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:52.359
another.

17
00:00:52.439 --> 00:00:56.863
Eliminating shell interpretation is more
reliable than attempting to escape every

18
00:00:56.943 --> 00:00:57.940
possible context.

19
00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.506
Treat shell-enabled execution as an
explicit security decision, not a convenience.

20
00:01:02.586 --> 00:01:05.453
If a requirement genuinely depends on
shell features,

21
00:01:05.533 --> 00:01:09.645
keep the entire command structure fixed in
trusted application code and do not

22
00:01:09.725 --> 00:01:11.532
interpolate request data into it.

23
00:01:11.612 --> 00:01:16.223
Prefer replacing shell pipelines with
direct processes connected through controlled

24
00:01:16.303 --> 00:01:18.422
streams or using an in-process library.

25
00:01:18.502 --> 00:01:20.371
Review deployment details as well:

26
00:01:20.451 --> 00:01:24.314
some platform-specific scripts require a
shell or another interpreter,

27
00:01:24.394 --> 00:01:27.635
so an API name alone does not prove how
the program is launched.

28
00:01:27.715 --> 00:01:31.516
Direct execution narrows risk but does not
make arguments trustworthy.

29
00:01:31.596 --> 00:01:35.210
The target utility may support flags that
write files, load plugins,

30
00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.408
access the network, or execute scripts.

31
00:01:37.488 --> 00:01:41.601
Executable selection, paths, environment
variables, privilege, lifetime,

32
00:01:41.681 --> 00:01:43.799
and output remain security-sensitive.

33
00:01:43.879 --> 00:01:46.995
Use execFile when bounded buffered output
is appropriate,

34
00:01:47.075 --> 00:01:49.879
or spawn when streaming and backpressure
are needed.

35
00:01:49.959 --> 00:01:54.258
In both cases, design a narrow operation
whose allowed arguments are known before

36
00:01:54.338 --> 00:01:55.460
any request arrives.
